.SH "Detailed Description"
.PP 

.PP
.nf
Backing implementation for InstallRequirement's various constructors

The idea here is that these formed a major chunk of InstallRequirement's size
so, moving them and support code dedicated to them outside of that class
helps creates for better understandability for the rest of the code\&.

These are meant to be used elsewhere within pip to create instances of
InstallRequirement\&.

.fi
.PP
 
.SH "Function Documentation"
.PP 
.SS " Optional[str] pip\&._internal\&.req\&.constructors\&._get_url_from_path (str path, str name)\fC [protected]\fP"

.PP
.nf
First, it checks whether a provided path is an installable directory\&. If it
is, returns the path\&.

If false, check if the path is an archive file (such as a \&.whl)\&.
The function checks if the path is a file\&. If false, if the path has
an @, it will treat it as a PEP 440 URL requirement and return the path\&.

.fi
.PP
 
.SS " bool pip\&._internal\&.req\&.constructors\&._looks_like_path (str name)\fC [protected]\fP"

.PP
.nf
Checks whether the string 'looks like' a path on the filesystem\&.

This does not check whether the target actually exists, only judge from the
appearance\&.

Returns true if any of the following conditions is true:
* a path separator is found (either os\&.path\&.sep or os\&.path\&.altsep);
* a dot is found (which represents the current directory)\&.

.fi
.PP
 
.SS " \fBRequirement\fP pip\&._internal\&.req\&.constructors\&._set_requirement_extras (\fBRequirement\fP req, Set[str] new_extras)\fC [protected]\fP"

.PP
.nf
Returns a new requirement based on the given one, with the supplied extras\&. If the
given requirement already has extras those are replaced (or dropped if no new extras
are given)\&.

.fi
.PP
 
.SS " Tuple[str, Optional[str]] pip\&._internal\&.req\&.constructors\&._strip_extras (str path)\fC [protected]\fP"

.SS " None pip\&._internal\&.req\&.constructors\&.check_first_requirement_in_file (str filename)"

.PP
.nf
Check if file is parsable as a requirements file\&.

This is heavily based on ``pkg_resources\&.parse_requirements``, but
simplified to just check the first meaningful line\&.

:raises InvalidRequirement: If the first meaningful line cannot be parsed
    as an requirement\&.

.fi
.PP
 
